// src/syscall.pdx -- shell.ENH-001 (paideia-os/shell#28)
//
// Syscall module: SC+ sysno constants + thin callable wrappers.
//
// =============================================================================
// SCOPE
// =============================================================================
//
// The shell's syscall floor. At v1.0.0 the shell repository contained
// ZERO `syscall` instructions -- every deferred item in `STATUS.md`
// (exec, wait, tty read, PdxFS write, audit send, broker send) was
// blocked on this one absence. ENH-001 lands the substrate that every
// stage in the v2.0 plan (`design/enhancement-plan.md` §4 Stage 0)
// consumes: a `Syscall` module owning the SC+ IDs the shell needs and
// a thin callable wrapper per ID, ready to be invoked from the higher-
// level modules (exec, line_reader, history, broker_bind, ...) as
// their `EX_STUB`/`LR_STUB`/etc. tails are de-stubbed in ENH-005/007/
// 008.
//
// This module lands the FLOOR only. No caller in the repository is
// wired up here -- that is ENH-002+. The exit criteria for #28 are:
//   1. `syscall` instructions appear in `src/`.
//   2. The ten SC+ IDs the shell needs are named symbolically
//      (extended from nine at shell#47: SYS_YIELD = 5 added for the
//      KIND_TTY(read) seam's TTY_READ_EMPTY yield-and-retry loop).
//   3. Each wrapper compiles clean under paideia-as >= 0.21.
//   4. The unit fingerprint (`tests/test_syscall_floor.pdx`) proves
//      the shim shape by exercising `sys_getcwd` + `sys_write` when
//      run against a live kernel.
//
// shell#44 addendum: the floor grew from nine to ten wrappers with the
// addition of SC+ 56 (sys_fork). The ENH-001 enumeration deliberately
// omitted fork; ENH-005 landed a "sys_execve replaces the shell"
// degenerate exec path with a documented §FORK GAP that shell#44
// retires by adding the fork wrapper here and making
// `exec_spawn_and_wait` fork-before-exec (child branch execs; parent
// branch waits + closes the audit record). The kernel-side fork body
// has been live since R15-M6-003 (paideia-os #554) with the R17-M0-724
// enqueue completion; see paideia-os `src/kernel/core/syscall/handlers/
// sys_fork.pdx` for the ground truth. The wrapper here matches the
// monorepo canonical shim (paideia-os `src/user/syscall_shim.pdx`
// `sys_fork`) byte-for-byte in its syscall body.
//
// =============================================================================
// DESIGN DECISION: shared module (not inlined per callsite)
// =============================================================================
//
// The monorepo has two established patterns for user-space syscalls:
//
//   (a) INLINE at every callsite. Used by single-file one-ELF binaries
//       (`src/user/cat.pdx:127`, `child_hello.pdx`, `true.pdx`,
//       `echo_client.pdx`). The rationale is self-containment: the ELF
//       stays small, no linker dependency on the shared shim, no
//       object-set collisions between `init.elf` and `shell.elf`.
//       Every one of these tools is a single .pdx file compiled to one
//       .elf; extending the pattern to a 12+-module program would
//       duplicate the sysno at N callsites and multiply the drift
//       surface against the frozen SC+ table.
//
//   (b) SHARED SHIM linked by multi-object-set consumers. The
//       canonical precedent is `src/user/syscall_shim.pdx` in the
//       paideia-os monorepo -- a 25-wrapper module linked by both
//       `init.elf` and (the kernel-tree) `shell.elf`. Each wrapper is
//       3 to 4 instructions (`mov rax, N; [shuffle if arity>=4];
//       syscall; ret`); the shared surface is what makes drift
//       auditable at a SINGLE point when the SC+ table evolves. This
//       Syscall module ports the same shape, restricted to the 9
//       sysnos the shell v2.0 plan enumerates.
//
// The shell repo has ALWAYS been multi-module (12 `.pdx` files at
// v1.0.0, each producing its own `.o` under `build-out/`, all called
// into a single `shell` ELF at link time). Cross-module `call`
// linkage is already the norm here -- every module calls
// `Shell.shell_note`. Introducing a shared Syscall wrapper set is
// architecturally consistent with the existing cross-module wiring
// and matches the multi-object-set precedent of `syscall_shim.pdx`.
// See `design/architecture.md` §2a for the recorded decision.
//
// =============================================================================
// CALLING CONVENTION
// =============================================================================
//
// paideia-as user callers reach these wrappers via SysV:
//   rdi = arg0, rsi = arg1, rdx = arg2, rcx = arg3, r8 = arg4, r9 = arg5
// The wrappers set `rax = SC+ ID` and issue `syscall`, which follows
// the Linux SYSCALL convention:
//   rdi = arg0, rsi = arg1, rdx = arg2, r10 = arg3, r8 = arg4, r9 = arg5
// For arity <= 3 no argument shuffle is needed (rdi/rsi/rdx pass
// through). For `sys_wait4` (arity 4) the wrapper prepends `mov r10,
// rcx` to move the SysV arg3 (rcx) into the SYSCALL arg3 slot (r10);
// `rcx` itself is clobbered by SYSCALL semantics on the CPU side, so
// the shuffle is safe to do at entry. See `syscall_shim.pdx`
// sys_wait4 body for the identical pattern.
//
// Every wrapper is a LEAF function: no push/pop, no local stack
// frame. The `ret` after `syscall` returns to the SysV caller with
// `rax` holding the kernel's return value (non-negative on success,
// negative errno on failure per `design/user/syscall-table.md`).
//
// `syscall` clobbers `rcx` and `r11` per x86-64 SYSCALL semantics --
// both are SysV caller-save, so no caller needs to preserve them
// across a wrapper call. All other GP registers are preserved by the
// kernel.
//
// =============================================================================
// PAIDEIA-AS CONFORMANCE
// =============================================================================
//
//   - Module name PascalCase basename (`Syscall`), no directory prefix.
//   - No `test` mnemonic; wrappers make no comparisons.
//   - No large immediates -- every sysno fits imm32.
//   - r11 is scratch (paideia-as reserved); wrappers do not touch r11.
//   - No byte reads.
//   - Every wrapper is a leaf function (no push/pop parity).
//   - Effect + capability annotations match `syscall_shim.pdx` for
//     the corresponding SC+ ID. See per-wrapper justification for the
//     paideia-os design source.

module Syscall = structure {

  // ==========================================================================
  // SC+ sysno constants
  //
  // Frozen at R15.M4 (paideia-os #536) with two later extensions:
  // R86.M1 landed 85/86 (chdir/getcwd) at paideia-os #1959/#1960; R62
  // froze the execve ABI (`design/user/execve-abi.md`) so 59 continues
  // to name the same handler post-argc/argv marshalling. The full
  // authoritative table lives at `design/user/syscall-table.md`
  // (paideia-os).
  //
  // The shell needs the following eleven wrappers (originally nine per
  // `design/enhancement-plan.md` §4 Stage 0; extended at shell#44 with
  // SYS_FORK = 56 for the fork-before-exec landing, and at shell#47 with
  // SYS_YIELD = 5 for the KIND_TTY(read) seam's yield-and-retry loop):
  //   0   read     (line_reader: byte-at-a-time TTY read; ENH-007)
  //   1   write    (line_reader: prompt echo, `write` builtin; ENH-007)
  //   2   open     (path-search for exec, `.pds` script open; ENH-005)
  //   3   close    (exec cleanup, `.pds` script close; ENH-005)
  //   5   yield    (line_reader: TTY_READ_EMPTY busy-poll; #47 -> #46)
  //   56  fork     (exec: split parent+child before execve; shell#44)
  //   59  execve   (exec: replace child image with argv[0]; ENH-005)
  //   60  exit     (Shell::shell_main normal termination; ENH-006)
  //   61  wait4    (exec: block on child exit code; ENH-005)
  //   78  getdents (builtin: help /bin auto-enumeration; shell#29-help)
  //   85  chdir    (builtin: cd; ENH-004)
  //   86  getcwd   (builtin: pwd, prompt render; ENH-004/006)
  //
  // SC+ ID 5 (sys_yield) provenance: the kernel-side substrate exists
  // as `sched_yield` (paideia-os src/kernel/core/sched/yield.pdx) and
  // is reachable today via KIND_SCHED_CTX OP_YIELD (op_code=5). Placing
  // the direct-syscall wrapper at sysno 5 keeps the shell's floor
  // aligned with the design/architecture.md §3.3 ledger which names
  // `sys_yield` at SC+ ID 5 as the standard hook the TTY_READ_EMPTY
  // (0xFFFFEC35) branch needs so the caller's blocking-read contract
  // is preserved after the KIND_TTY(read) seam swap lands (#46). No
  // paideia-os design/user/syscall-table.md row exists at ID 5 today
  // (the frozen R15.M4 table's contiguous 0..4 block ends at
  // cap_invoke; the shell#47 landing reserves the shell-visible slot
  // in advance of the kernel-side dispatcher wiring). Until the kernel
  // dispatch grows a `cmp rdi, 5; je dispatch_yield` arm, callers
  // invoking sys_yield will observe -ENOSYS (0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFDA) --
  // acceptable at the floor-landing tempo because #47 has no in-tree
  // caller (the #46 KIND_TTY(read) migration is where the wrapper is
  // first exercised, and that landing pairs a kernel-side dispatch
  // arm with the wrapper's first call site).
  // ==========================================================================
  pub let SYS_READ    : u64 = 0
  pub let SYS_WRITE   : u64 = 1
  pub let SYS_OPEN    : u64 = 2
  pub let SYS_CLOSE   : u64 = 3
  pub let SYS_YIELD   : u64 = 5
  pub let SYS_FORK    : u64 = 56
  pub let SYS_EXECVE  : u64 = 59
  pub let SYS_EXIT    : u64 = 60
  pub let SYS_WAIT4   : u64 = 61
  pub let SYS_GETDENTS: u64 = 78
  pub let SYS_CHDIR   : u64 = 85
  pub let SYS_GETCWD  : u64 = 86

  // ==========================================================================
  // sys_read(fd, buf, count) -> u64
  //
  // Bytes read on success (0 == EOF), or a negative errno.
  // SysV rdi/rsi/rdx -> SYSCALL rdi/rsi/rdx (no shuffle; arity 3 < 4).
  // Kernel body: paideia-os `src/kernel/core/syscall/handlers/sys_read.pdx`.
  // Effect+cap taxonomy mirrors syscall_shim.pdx sys_read.
  // ==========================================================================
  pub let sys_read : (u64, u64, u64) -> u64 !{mem, sysreg} @{fs} =
    fn (fd: u64) (buf: u64) (count: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem, sysreg}, capabilities: {fs},
      justification: "shell.ENH-001 (#28): SC+ ID 0 wrapper. SysV rdi/rsi/rdx pass through unchanged to SYSCALL rdi/rsi/rdx (no arg3 shuffle: arity 3 < 4). Kernel body at paideia-os src/kernel/core/syscall/handlers/sys_read.pdx (landed R15.M4-004). Effect and capability taxonomy match the monorepo canonical shim at src/user/syscall_shim.pdx sys_read: !{mem, sysreg} for the SYSCALL side-effect + memory-touch (kernel writes count bytes into buf) and @{fs} because sys_read is fs-gated by every kernel VFS handler. Leaf function; syscall clobbers rcx/r11 (both SysV caller-save, harmless). Labels: none (linear body).",
      block: {
        mov rax, 0;
        syscall;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // sys_write(fd, buf, count) -> u64
  //
  // Bytes written on success, or a negative errno.
  // SysV rdi/rsi/rdx -> SYSCALL rdi/rsi/rdx (no shuffle).
  // Kernel body: paideia-os `src/kernel/core/syscall/handlers/sys_write.pdx`.
  // ==========================================================================
  pub let sys_write : (u64, u64, u64) -> u64 !{mem, sysreg} @{fs} =
    fn (fd: u64) (buf: u64) (count: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem, sysreg}, capabilities: {fs},
      justification: "shell.ENH-001 (#28): SC+ ID 1 wrapper. SysV rdi/rsi/rdx pass through (arity 3). Kernel body at paideia-os src/kernel/core/syscall/handlers/sys_write.pdx (landed R15.M4-004 #538). Effect and capability set match syscall_shim.pdx sys_write. The shell will invoke this from at least three consumer paths at v2.0: the line reader's prompt echo (ENH-007), the `write`/`echo` builtin bodies (ENH-004/006), and CommandRecord's audit-first pre-flush (ENH-005). Leaf function.",
      block: {
        mov rax, 1;
        syscall;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // sys_open(path, flags, mode) -> u64
  //
  // fd on success (>= 0), or a negative errno.
  // SysV rdi/rsi/rdx -> SYSCALL rdi/rsi/rdx (no shuffle).
  // Kernel body: paideia-os `src/kernel/core/syscall/handlers/sys_open.pdx`.
  // ==========================================================================
  pub let sys_open : (u64, u64, u64) -> u64 !{mem, sysreg} @{fs} =
    fn (path: u64) (flags: u64) (mode: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem, sysreg}, capabilities: {fs},
      justification: "shell.ENH-001 (#28): SC+ ID 2 wrapper. SysV rdi/rsi/rdx pass through (arity 3). Kernel body at paideia-os src/kernel/core/syscall/handlers/sys_open.pdx (landed R16.M3-001). Two shell consumers at v2.0: (a) exec's path-search over the InitCap-seeded PATH cap set (ENH-005) opens each candidate binary path to test existence, (b) Pds.M2's `.pds` script header parser reads the script file's header bytes to populate PdsHeader (ENH-005 substrate integration). Leaf function.",
      block: {
        mov rax, 2;
        syscall;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // sys_close(fd) -> u64
  //
  // 0 on success, or a negative errno.
  // SysV rdi -> SYSCALL rdi (no shuffle).
  // Kernel body: paideia-os `src/kernel/core/syscall/handlers/sys_close.pdx`.
  // Effect set trimmed relative to sys_read/write: no memory touch on the
  // kernel side; still `sysreg` for SYSCALL itself and `fs` for the vnode-
  // pool release.
  // ==========================================================================
  pub let sys_close : (u64) -> u64 !{sysreg} @{fs} =
    fn (fd: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {sysreg}, capabilities: {fs},
      justification: "shell.ENH-001 (#28): SC+ ID 3 wrapper. SysV rdi passes through (arity 1). Kernel body at paideia-os src/kernel/core/syscall/handlers/sys_close.pdx (landed R16.M3-002). Effect drops `mem` relative to sys_read/write because sys_close does not touch user memory; sysreg + fs stay per syscall_shim.pdx sys_close. Shell consumers: exec cleanup of open fds prior to sys_execve (ENH-005) and Pds body-file close after header parse (ENH-005). Leaf function.",
      block: {
        mov rax, 3;
        syscall;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // sys_yield() -> u64
  //
  // Voluntary CPU release (cooperative multitasking hint). Returns
  // 0 (YIELD_OK) or 1 (YIELD_NOOP: pick_next selected the same TCB)
  // on success, or a negative errno / -ENOSYS while the kernel-side
  // dispatch arm for sysno 5 is unwired. Arity 0 -- no SysV args, no
  // shuffle.
  //
  // Consumer at v2.0: the LineReader's KIND_TTY(read) migration
  // (shell#46) will wrap the seam's TTY_READ_EMPTY (0xFFFFEC35)
  // branch in a `sys_yield; jmp poll_top` loop so the caller's
  // blocking-read contract is preserved (see
  // design/architecture.md §3.3 pseudocode block). Until #46 lands
  // the wrapper is unused; the floor addition here (#47) is the
  // dependency #46 step 3 blocks on.
  //
  // Kernel body: paideia-os `src/kernel/core/sched/yield.pdx`
  // `sched_yield`. Today the syscall dispatcher (paideia-os
  // src/kernel/core/syscall/dispatch.pdx) does NOT carry a
  // `cmp rdi, 5; je dispatch_yield` arm, so a direct syscall with
  // rax=5 falls through to `dispatch_enosys` (-ENOSYS). The paired
  // kernel-side landing is expected to wire dispatch to a leaf
  // wrapper that tail-calls `sched_yield` -- see the R13 legacy
  // `sys_yield` (paideia-os design/audit/entries/r13-m5-003-
  // syscall-table.md row 1) for the canonical shape (Rev-1 dropped
  // the R13 assignment during the R17 SC+ freeze because no caller
  // existed; the #46 seam is that caller, and the shell#47 wrapper
  // reserves the shell's slot at the ID design/architecture.md §3.3
  // named).
  // ==========================================================================
  pub let sys_yield : () -> u64 !{sysreg} @{sched} =
    fn () -> unsafe {
      effects: {sysreg}, capabilities: {sched},
      justification: "shell.ENH-001 extension (#47): SC+ ID 5 wrapper. Arity 0 -- no SysV args, no arg shuffle. Kernel body at paideia-os src/kernel/core/sched/yield.pdx `sched_yield` (landed R10-m3-002 / R9-m3-003 refactor); the kernel-side dispatcher arm for sysno 5 is expected to land alongside shell#46's KIND_TTY(read) seam migration so the wrapper's first in-tree caller (the TTY_READ_EMPTY yield-and-retry loop inside `lr_read_one_byte`) exercises a real handler. Effect and capability set match the R13 legacy sys_yield ({sysreg} + @{sched}) and the kernel-side KIND_SCHED_CTX OP_YIELD posture (paideia-os src/kernel/core/cap/kind_sched.pdx `kind_sched_invoke`): sysreg for SYSCALL itself, @{sched} because the handler tail-calls into sched_pick_next / sched_switch. No `mem` effect -- yield touches no user memory. Leaf function; SYSCALL clobbers rcx/r11 (both SysV caller-save, harmless to the wrapper's siblings). Provenance for SC+ ID 5: design/architecture.md §3.3 (this repo) names sys_yield at ID 5 as the standard hook for the KIND_TTY(read) seam's blocking-read contract preservation; paideia-os design/user/syscall-table.md carries no row at ID 5 today, so #47 reserves the shell-visible slot ahead of the kernel dispatch wiring (matches ENH-001's floor-first / consumer-later staging pattern -- see sibling sys_execve/sys_wait4 landing which preceded ENH-005's exec-path caller by one issue).",
      block: {
        mov rax, 5;
        syscall;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // sys_fork() -> u64  (shell#44)
  //
  // Split the calling task into parent+child. Returns:
  //   parent:  child pid (u32 zero-extended to u64, > 0)
  //   child:   0
  //   either:  a negative errno on failure (-EAGAIN if the pid pool /
  //            aspace_create ran out; -ENOMEM if the copy-on-write
  //            page-table clone OOM'd)
  //
  // Arity 0 -- no SysV -> SYSCALL argument shuffle. Kernel body at
  // paideia-os `src/kernel/core/syscall/handlers/sys_fork.pdx`:
  // `sys_fork_body` produces the child slab via task_new + aspace_clone
  // _cow + fd_table copy, materialises the child's ring-3 landing (task
  // state, rip/rsp/rflags snapshotted from the parent's syscall frame),
  // then runq_enqueues the child. The kernel materialises the child's
  // return value (0 in rax) at `sys_fork_child_landing` on the child's
  // first schedule -- the wrapper here is agnostic to which side it
  // returns to; the value in rax discriminates.
  //
  // Effect / capability set matches paideia-os syscall_shim.pdx
  // sys_fork: {mem, sysreg} + @{sched, mem}. `mem` because the copy-on-
  // write clone touches every reachable user PTE (page-table walk on
  // the parent's aspace + install on the child's aspace); `sysreg` for
  // the SYSCALL instruction; @{sched} because the child is enqueued on
  // the runqueue; @{mem} because the aspace is duplicated. `fs` is NOT
  // in the cap set -- the fd table is copied but no VFS walk is issued
  // by fork itself; the fd inheritance-hold is a refcount++ on
  // already-open vnodes.
  //
  // Sole shell consumer as of shell#44: exec_spawn_and_wait's fork-
  // before-exec head. Splitting execve into fork+execve (POSIX shape)
  // is what makes sys_wait4 structurally reachable on the parent
  // timeline; prior to this landing the shell's exec path was a
  // degenerate "sys_execve replaces the shell process itself" pattern
  // (see the retired §FORK GAP block in src/exec.pdx). Leaf function.
  //
  // Return-value shape gotcha: the kernel writes the child's pid to
  // BOTH rax (parent-visible) AND rdx (companion slot per the fork
  // return-value convention that paideia-os follows -- rax has the pid
  // for the parent, rdx has 0 as a marker). The wrapper does not need
  // to consume rdx; it returns rax to its SysV caller as a u64. A
  // caller that wants to discriminate parent from child in constant
  // time can compare rax against 0 (child) vs > 0 (parent).
  // ==========================================================================
  pub let sys_fork : () -> u64 !{mem, sysreg} @{sched, mem} =
    fn () -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem, sysreg}, capabilities: {sched, mem},
      justification: "shell#44: SC+ ID 56 wrapper. Arity 0 -- no arg shuffle. Kernel body at paideia-os src/kernel/core/syscall/handlers/sys_fork.pdx (landed R15-M6-003 #554; child-schedule materialisation R17-M0-724-D6 #724). Return convention: parent gets child pid in rax (u32 zero-extended); child gets 0 in rax (materialised by sys_fork_child_landing on the child's first schedule); either side gets a negative errno (-EAGAIN 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF5 on pid-pool/aspace_create OOM; -ENOMEM 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF4 on cow-clone leaf OOM). Effect + capability set matches paideia-os syscall_shim.pdx sys_fork byte-for-byte: {mem, sysreg} for the SYSCALL + the copy-on-write PTE walk; @{sched, mem} for runqueue enqueue + aspace duplication. `fs` deliberately absent -- fork copies the fd table (32 qwords) and refcount-holds the inherited vnodes but issues no VFS walk. Sole shell consumer at shell#44: exec_spawn_and_wait's fork-before-exec head (retires the ENH-005 §FORK GAP by making sys_wait4 structurally reachable on the parent timeline; child branch execs, parent branch waits + closes the audit record). Leaf function; syscall clobbers rcx/r11 (both SysV caller-save, harmless). Labels: none (linear body).",
      block: {
        mov rax, 56;
        syscall;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // sys_execve(path, argv, envp) -> u64
  //
  // Does NOT return on success (kernel replaces the current image); on
  // failure returns a negative errno. The frozen ABI at R62 populates
  // rdi/rsi/rdx with argc/argv/envp for the callee at `_start` -- see
  // `design/user/execve-abi.md` (paideia-os). This wrapper matches
  // the CALLING side's ABI: `path` in rdi, `argv` in rsi, `envp` in
  // rdx; the shim marshals them into the new image's rdi/rsi/rdx per
  // the frozen contract.
  //
  // SysV rdi/rsi/rdx -> SYSCALL rdi/rsi/rdx (no shuffle).
  // Kernel body: paideia-os `src/kernel/core/syscall/handlers/sys_execve_shim.pdx`.
  // ==========================================================================
  pub let sys_execve : (u64, u64, u64) -> u64 !{mem, sysreg} @{sched, mem} =
    fn (path: u64) (argv: u64) (envp: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem, sysreg}, capabilities: {sched, mem},
      justification: "shell.ENH-001 (#28): SC+ ID 59 wrapper. SysV rdi/rsi/rdx pass through (arity 3). Kernel body at paideia-os src/kernel/core/syscall/handlers/sys_execve_shim.pdx (landed R15.M6, argv/envp marshalling completed R62 per design/user/execve-abi.md). Effect and capability set match syscall_shim.pdx sys_execve: {mem, sysreg} for SYSCALL + argv/envp marshalling on the way in, @{sched, mem} because execve replaces the address space (mem cap) and reschedules the child image (sched cap). The wrapper does not return on the success path -- the kernel loads the new ELF's _start and control never comes back to the caller; the negative-errno path DOES return (bad path, bad argv, etc.) so the wrapper is still typed as `-> u64`. Sole shell consumer at v2.0: exec_spawn_and_wait's post-narrow-caps invocation (ENH-005). Leaf function.",
      block: {
        mov rax, 59;
        syscall;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // sys_exit(status) -> u64
  //
  // Never returns. Return type is `u64` for signature uniformity with
  // the other wrappers; every caller must treat the call as
  // unreachable after the syscall issue.
  // SysV rdi -> SYSCALL rdi (no shuffle).
  // Kernel body: paideia-os `src/kernel/core/syscall/handlers/sys_exit.pdx`.
  // ==========================================================================
  pub let sys_exit : (u64) -> u64 !{sysreg} @{sched} =
    fn (status: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {sysreg}, capabilities: {sched},
      justification: "shell.ENH-001 (#28): SC+ ID 60 wrapper. SysV rdi passes through (arity 1). Kernel body at paideia-os src/kernel/core/syscall/handlers/sys_exit.pdx (landed R15.M4-003 #537). Effect and capability set match syscall_shim.pdx sys_exit: sysreg + @{sched} (task teardown reschedules; no user-memory touch). sys_exit does not return; the wrapper's `ret` is unreachable at run time and exists only to satisfy paideia-as's function-shape checker (every function must end in a return-shaped terminator). Sole shell consumer at v2.0: Shell::shell_main's EOF branch (ENH-006). Leaf function.",
      block: {
        mov rax, 60;
        syscall;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // sys_wait4(pid, wstatus, options, rusage) -> u64
  //
  // Reaped pid on success, or a negative errno. ARITY 4 -- requires
  // a `mov r10, rcx` prefix to shuffle the SysV arg3 (rcx) into the
  // SYSCALL arg3 slot (r10). See syscall_shim.pdx sys_wait4 body for
  // the identical pattern.
  //
  // SysV rdi/rsi/rdx/rcx -> SYSCALL rdi/rsi/rdx/r10.
  // Kernel body: paideia-os `src/kernel/core/syscall/handlers/sys_wait.pdx`.
  // ==========================================================================
  pub let sys_wait4 : (u64, u64, u64, u64) -> u64 !{mem, sysreg} @{sched} =
    fn (pid: u64) (wstatus: u64) (options: u64) (rusage: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem, sysreg}, capabilities: {sched},
      justification: "shell.ENH-001 (#28): SC+ ID 61 wrapper. SysV rdi/rsi/rdx/rcx must be shuffled to SYSCALL rdi/rsi/rdx/r10 -- SYSCALL clobbers rcx as the return-address slot, so arg3 lives in r10 on the raw entry side (Linux SYSCALL convention, paideia-os matches). The `mov r10, rcx` prefix is safe at wrapper entry: rcx has not yet been touched by anything, and the caller passes it verbatim per SysV. Kernel body at paideia-os src/kernel/core/syscall/handlers/sys_wait.pdx (landed R15.M6). Effect and capability set match syscall_shim.pdx sys_wait4: {mem, sysreg} + @{sched}; the mem effect covers the kernel writing wstatus/rusage back to the caller's buffers. Sole shell consumer at v2.0: exec_spawn_and_wait's blocking wait after execve (ENH-005). Leaf function. Labels: none.",
